-# Alcohol and color intensity in the complete UCI Wine dataset: pooled and cultivar-conditioned associations
+# Positive alcohol–color-intensity associations before and after cultivar adjustment in the complete UCI Wine dataset
 
 *Revised scientific draft.*
 
 ## Abstract
 
-We described the association between alcohol content and color intensity in all 178 records of the original UCI Wine dataset, overall and conditional on its three cultivar classes. Pearson and Spearman correlations and ordinary least-squares regressions summarized direction and magnitude. The pooled Pearson correlation was 0.546 and Spearman correlation was 0.635; the pooled slope was 1.560 recorded color-intensity units per recorded alcohol unit (nominal 95% HC3 normal-approximation interval, 1.246–1.875). Class-specific slopes were 1.094, 0.464 and 1.527. With class-specific intercepts, the common slope was 0.940 (0.492–1.389), and the class-adjusted partial correlation was 0.319. All single-record omissions retained positive pooled and adjusted slopes. Thus, positive direction was shared across classes, while class conditioning reduced the pooled magnitude. The differing class estimates and an interaction reference p-value of 0.106 do not establish equal slopes. These findings describe this historical dataset; intervals and reference tests are model-based sensitivity summaries and support neither population nor causal claims.
+Alcohol content and color intensity were positively associated overall and within all three cultivar classes in the original UCI Wine dataset. We quantified this pattern in all 178 records using Pearson and Spearman correlations and ordinary least-squares regressions, then assessed the association after cultivar-class adjustment. The pooled Pearson correlation was 0.546 and Spearman correlation was 0.635; the pooled slope was 1.560 recorded color-intensity units per recorded alcohol unit (nominal 95% HC3 normal-approximation interval, 1.246–1.875). Class-specific slopes were 1.094, 0.464 and 1.527. With class-specific intercepts, the common slope was 0.940 (0.492–1.389), and the class-adjusted partial correlation was 0.319. All single-record omissions retained positive pooled and adjusted slopes. Thus, positive direction was shared across classes, while class conditioning reduced the pooled magnitude. The differing class estimates and an interaction reference p-value of 0.106 do not establish equal slopes. These findings describe this historical dataset; intervals and reference tests are model-based sensitivity summaries and support neither population nor causal claims.
 
 ## Introduction
 
-The UCI Wine dataset contains chemical measurements from wines grown in one Italian region and derived from three cultivars [1]. Although the dataset has been used for classification, the present question concerns the relationship between two recorded measurements: alcohol content and color intensity. A pooled association combines variation within cultivar classes with differences among classes, so it need not describe the association within each class.
+The UCI Wine dataset contains chemical measurements from wines grown in one Italian region and derived from three cultivars [1]. We examined the relationship between its recorded alcohol content and color intensity. A pooled association combines variation within cultivar classes with differences among classes, so it need not describe the association within each class.
 
-We estimated the direction and magnitude of the alcohol–color-intensity association in the complete original dataset, then examined its direction and magnitude within cultivar class and after class adjustment. The aim was a coherent description of these historical records, with no claim about wine quality, a causal alcohol effect or a wider wine population.
