 ## Discussion
 
-Alcohol content and color intensity were positively associated in this dataset, both overall and within each of its three cultivar classes. The association persisted after class adjustment, with a smaller common slope and partial correlation than their pooled counterparts. Cultivar conditioning therefore changes the magnitude of the descriptive relationship without reversing its direction. The pooled slope alone would give an incomplete account of the within-class association.
+Alcohol content and color intensity were positively associated in this dataset, both overall and within each of its three cultivar classes. The association persisted after class adjustment, with a smaller common slope and partial correlation than their pooled counterparts. Cultivar conditioning reveals a smaller within-class summary while preserving the positive direction. Reporting both pooled and class-conditioned estimates therefore gives a fuller description of the relationship.
 
 Consistency of direction should be distinguished from equality of magnitude. The three fitted slopes differ numerically, and the adjusted common slope is a weighted summary rather than a demonstration of identical class responses. The interaction diagnostic supplies neither an equivalence test nor a population-level conclusion. Rank correlations and the omission analyses support the positive descriptive direction, while the wide quadratic interval does not establish linearity.
 
-The original records lack a documented probability sampling frame, collection dates, batch structure, replication details and measurement units. HC3 addresses heterogeneous error variance under independent errors; it cannot remedy unknown clustering, measurement error or an inappropriate mean model. Accordingly, these coefficients summarize the recorded wines and do not identify a causal mechanism, establish practical wine-quality implications or justify extrapolation to other wines. Within that scope, the complete-data analyses answer the original question: the alcohol–color-intensity association is positive overall and conditional on cultivar class, with attenuation after class adjustment and unresolved equality of class-specific magnitudes.
